From 3fa3f7e2dc3eeeeab2c222dbf48c3a987f374567 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 23:28:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] feat(windows): spklib builds and runs on Windows -- a
 platform layer, Windows paths and Capture One for Windows' roots

What differs between macOS, Linux and Windows now lives in src/platform.hpp:
file times (st_mtimespec / st_mtim / GetFileAttributesExW), gmtime/localtime/
timegm, a rename that never replaces (renamex_np RENAME_EXCL / MoveFileExW
without REPLACE_EXISTING), Unicode NFC (CoreFoundation / NormalizeString),
and how a path is spelled. On macOS and Linux nothing changes: path_string is
path::string() and every helper keeps the old arithmetic for a "/" root.

Windows:
- Paths are kept and returned with '/' separators ("C:/Users/me/A.NEF",
  "//server/share/..."), which every API spklib calls accepts. join_path,
  is_prefix_dir, ensure_location and resolve know a root can be "C:/".
- Capture One for Windows writes ZWINROOT "C:\" and ZMACROOT '', with '\'
  in ZRELATIVEPATH (read from a catalog Capture One 16.6.4 for Windows made
  for this). c1::platform_root picks ZWINROOT on Windows (ZMACROOT when
  there is none, as in a catalog made on a Mac) and the relative path's
  separators are converted; off Windows ZMACROOT is the root, as before.
- The read-only snapshot opens file:///C:/... (and file:////server/...).
- The snapshot copy reads with CreateFileW sharing read/write/delete, and
  writes the SQLite lock-byte page (1 GiB) as zeros instead of reading it:
  SQLite never uses that page, and while Capture One holds a write lock a
  read of it fails (Windows locks are mandatory). A copy shorter than the
  catalog is now a retry on every platform, not a quick_check failure.
- The host puts stdin/stdout in binary mode: replies end in "\n".
- spklib_create refuses to start unless the active code page is UTF-8
  (the manifest the Windows build embeds), rather than mangle a non-ASCII
  path in a narrow-string API.

Tests: census() and the fixture's ZPATHLOCATION rows are portable (Windows
rows as Capture One for Windows writes them); paths are compared in
spklib's spelling; the real-catalog oracle resolves ZWINROOT on Windows and
copies the database with plain reads (CopyFile cannot open a file Capture
One has open for writing). New: path helpers, platform_root/resolve for both
platforms, the lock-byte page copy (on Windows under a real LockFileEx),
rename-no-replace, and a catalog in a non-ASCII folder. The NFC lookup test
and the rename test say "skipped" on Linux, where NFC is the identity and
rename(2) replaces.

Gate: Linux (g++ 13, system SQLite 3.45.1): spklib-tests -- 521 passed, 0
failed. Not yet built on Windows in this commit.

